  "worked_solution": "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
